    "case_id": "583",
    "case_summary": "A previously healthy 18-year-old female patient, who was an experienced volleyball player and regularly participated in yoga and weightlifting, presented to the emergency department after her eighth surfing lesson. While walking to her vehicle, she developed lower back pain and a tingling sensation radiating along the posterior aspect of her bilateral lower extremities. Within 20 minutes, she developed bilateral lower-extremity weakness and subsequently was unable to step out of the vehicle independently.\n\nInitial examination showed:- 0/5 strength of the bilateral lower extremities (MRC scale)- Intact sensation and proprioceptionFollow-up examination (5 days after injury):- Lower-extremity strength: 1 to 2/5 on MRC scale- Positive Babinski reflex on the right- Clonus bilaterally- Intact rectal sensation and bowel continence- Classified as L1 American Spinal Injury Association Impairment Scale (AIS) C\n\n- Imaging Studies:  1. MRI of thoracic spine:     - Abnormal T2 cord signal from T8 to T9 disc level downwards to the conus medullaris     - No significant contrast enhancement     - Diffusion-weighted imaging was not conducted  2. Spinal angiogram:     - No abnormal findings noted  - Laboratory Tests:  - CSF analysis: negative for inflammatory/infectious etiologies  - Serum analysis: negative for inflammatory/infectious etiologies",
